WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.862
Until the container is wired, no payment goes through.

2
00:00:05.862 --> 00:00:10.137
Here is one start, from the first line of main to the first payment done.

3
00:00:10.367 --> 00:00:13.251
The reflective container does everything now.

4
00:00:13.251 --> 00:00:19.916
It finds the components, reads their annotations, orders the graph, and has Byte Buddy write a subclass.

5
00:00:20.133 --> 00:00:23.760
Ready after 1,029 milliseconds.

6
00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:26.568
It loaded 2,207 classes.

7
00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:33.183
The same app, wired by generated code, loads 991 classes.

8
00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:37.161
So 1,216 classes are pure machinery.

9
00:00:37.367 --> 00:00:45.117
814 come from byte-buddy.jar: Byte Buddy's own machinery, loaded before it can write one subclass.

10
00:00:45.333 --> 00:00:52.295
Then the 3 classes it writes, 30 JDK proxy classes, and 123 hidden classes.

11
00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:58.715
Each one is found, parsed and verified, then run in the interpreter, because nothing is hot yet.

12
00:00:59.533 --> 00:01:04.616
None of this depends on anything but the classes, and those are fixed when you build.

13
00:01:04.616 --> 00:01:07.547
So run the same steps on the build machine, once.

14
00:01:07.767 --> 00:01:11.613
A cycle in the graph now fails the build instead of the deploy.

15
00:01:11.833 --> 00:01:20.757
The generator writes plain Java: a holder class for each singleton, and the interceptors as source, with the annotation values written in.

16
00:01:20.967 --> 00:01:21.558
Predict.

17
00:01:21.558 --> 00:01:24.073
What is left for each start to do?

18
00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:27.103
Only the holders.

19
00:01:27.103 --> 00:01:34.923
The JVM initializes each one the first time get touches it, once, under the class initialization lock.

20
00:01:35.133 --> 00:01:41.945
Ready after 266 milliseconds, with 991 classes, and no reflection.

21
00:01:42.467 --> 00:01:50.431
On a busy machine the times moved by a factor of two between runs, but the reflective start was always several times slower.

22
00:01:51.233 --> 00:01:53.878
Here is one holder, in Java and Kotlin.

23
00:01:53.878 --> 00:01:57.540
A plain new call, with its dependency's holder inside.

24
00:01:57.767 --> 00:01:58.413
The price.

25
00:01:58.413 --> 00:02:01.388
The set of components is fixed when you build.

26
00:02:01.388 --> 00:02:05.851
And a holder whose constructor throws stays failed until you restart.

27
00:02:06.667 --> 00:02:07.295
The rule.

28
00:02:07.295 --> 00:02:11.481
Work that depends only on the classes belongs at build time.

29
00:02:11.481 --> 00:02:14.132
A start should run only ordinary code.

30
00:02:14.333 --> 00:02:15.373
Check yourself.

31
00:02:15.373 --> 00:02:20.363
In the generated container, where does @Retry's 3 come from at run time?

32
00:02:23.067 --> 00:02:24.795
From the generated source.

33
00:02:24.795 --> 00:02:29.181
The generator read @Retry at build time and wrote 3 into the code.
